BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Short courses in health economics 2026
DTSTART:20260526T093000
DTEND:20260527T170000
DESCRIPTION:The <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/che">Centre for 
 Health Economics (CHE)</a> offers regular short courses.\n\nThese courses 
 aim to increase knowledge of health economics and health economic methods\
 , particularly in their application to the Australian health care system.\
 n\n<strong>We recommend completing our two May 2026 courses concurrently. 
 A discount is available for participants who purchase both the Economics o
 f Primary Care and the Economics of Mixed Health Systems courses in a sing
 le transaction.</strong>\n\n<!-- false -->\n<div class="accordion nested-a
 ccordion js-frontend-complex-accordion" id="Accordion-3981177" role="tabli
 st">\n<div class="hidden">\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__expand-t
 ext">View</span>\n<span class="hidden nested-accordion__collapse_text">Clo
 se</span>\n</div>\n<section class="panel section1 Economics_of_Primary_Car
 e_26_May_2026-1 close">\n<h3 aria-controls="Economics_of_Primary_Care_26_M
 ay_2026-1" aria-expanded="false" class="accTitle" data-click-event="monash
 .accordion.toggle" data-expanded-by-default="false" data-position="1" data
 -title="Economics of Primary Care (26 May 2026)" data-type="nested" id="Ec
 onomics_of_Primary_Care_26_May_2026-1" role="tab">\n<span class="accTitle_
 _icon"><svg><use href="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/git_bridge/000
 9/2651868/dist/assets/images/icon-spritesheet.svg?h=0444725a#calendar-2"><
 /use></svg></span>\n<span class="accTitle__text">Economics of Primary Care
  (26 May 2026)</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-wrapper">\n<a class="
 accTitle__toggle" href="#Economics_of_Primary_Care_26_May_2026-1" rel="1">
 \n<span class="accTitle__toggle-text">View</span>\n<span class="accTitle__
 toggle-arrow"><img alt="View" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/fi
 le/0011/2179901/arrow-outline-right.svg"/></span>\n</a>\n</span>\n</h3>\n<
 div aria-labelledby="Economics_of_Primary_Care_26_May_2026-1" class="accCo
 ntent" role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div class="accContent__i
 nner">\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div id="component_3981182">\n<
 h3>Date and time</h3><p>Tuesday\, 26 May 2026\, 9.30am – 5pm</p><h3>Loc
 ation</h3><p>Monash College\, 750 Collins St\, Docklands VIC 3008</p><h3>O
 verview</h3><p>Our Economics of Primary Care course introduces participant
 s to evidence on the key economic issues facing primary care. The course c
 ontent covers the role and impact of primary care in health systems\, the 
 effects of different financing and payment models on primary care\, and th
 e economics of the primary care workforce.</p><p>The course will explain k
 ey economic concepts and equip participants with up-to-date understanding 
 of how economists think about primary care and the relevant economic evide
 nce. Participants will be guided through worked examples demonstrating how
  and why economics can be applied to improve policy and practice.</p><h3>L
 earning outcomes</h3><p>At the end of the course\, participants will:</p><
 ul type="square"><li>Understand how economists think about key issues rele
 vant to primary care.</li><li>Be familiar with the latest high-quality aca
 demic empirical evidence.</li><li>Be able to use economic thinking to help
  improve policy and practice.</li><li>Be able to analyse and predict the i
 ntended and unintended effects of policy interventions.</li></ul><h3>Who s
 hould attend?</h3><p>The courses are suitable for those who want to increa
 se their familiarity with the application of economic concepts and economi
 c thinking to current policy issues and debates using the latest evidence.
   No previous knowledge of economics is required\, and the material will
  be presented in a non-technical way.</p><p>The courses will be suitable f
 or:</p><ul><li>Public servants and health policy analysts working for gove
 rnment and non-government agencies.</li><li>Health service managers across
  the public and private health sectors.</li><li>Health professionals.</li>
 <li>Consultants working in the health and human services sector.</li></ul>
 <h3>Cost</h3><ul><li>$950AUD for a single course\, or $1800AUD for the two
 -course package. <ul><li><strong>Volume discounts are also available for m
 ore than three participants</strong> from the same organisation. Please <a
  href="https://www.monash.edu/business/che/contact-us">contact us</a> dire
 ctly for further information about how to take advantage of the volume dis
 counts.</li></ul><p>Fees include attendance at a full day of face-to-face 
 seminars and group work\, catered lunch\, morning and afternoon tea.</p></
 li></ul><h3>Teaching staff</h3><div style="overflow: auto"><h4><a href="ht
 tps://research.monash.edu/en/persons/anthony-scott"><img alt="Anthony Scot
 t" height="175" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/image/0007/37047
 22/sq-anthony-scott.jpg" style="float: right\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" 
 width="175"/>Professor Anthony Scott</a>\, Director\, Centre for Health Ec
 onomics\, Monash Business School\, Monash University</h4><p>Prof Scott is 
 an elected Fellow of the Academy of the Social Sciences in Australia\, a P
 ast President of the Australian Health Economics Society\, and was a Board
  Director of the International Health Economics Association. His research 
 interests are in the health care labour markets\, physician behaviour\, an
 d healthcare financing and incentives.</p></div><h3>Program</h3><table id=
 "table68478"><thead><tr><th id="table68478r1c1">Time</th><th id="table6847
 8r1c2">Session</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td headers="table68478r1c1">\n
