 nitix.com/centre-for-health-economics-short-courses-26-and-27-may" target=
 "_blank">Enrol now</a></p>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</div>\n</section>\n<s
 ection class="panel section2 Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_May_2026
 -2">\n<h3 aria-controls="Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_May_2026-2" 
 aria-expanded="false" class="accTitle" data-click-event="monash.accordion.
 toggle" data-position="2" data-title="Economics of Mixed Health Systems (2
 7 May 2026)" data-type="nested" id="Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_M
 ay_2026-2" role="tab">\n<span class="accTitle__icon"><svg><use href="https
 ://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/git_bridge/0009/2651868/dist/assets/images
 /icon-spritesheet.svg?h=0444725a#calendar-2"></use></svg></span>\n<span cl
 ass="accTitle__text">Economics of Mixed Health Systems (27 May 2026)</span
 >\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-wrapper">\n<a class="accTitle__toggle" hr
 ef="#Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_May_2026-2" rel="2">\n<span clas
 s="accTitle__toggle-text">View</span>\n<span class="accTitle__toggle-arrow
 "><img alt="View" src="https://www.monash.edu/__data/assets/file/0011/2179
 901/arrow-outline-right.svg"/></span>\n</a>\n</span>\n</h3>\n<div aria-lab
 elledby="Economics_of_Mixed_Health_Systems_27_May_2026-2" class="accConten
 t" role="tabpanel" style="display: none\;">\n<div class="accContent__inner
 ">\n<div class="accContent__wrapper">\n<div id="component_3981186">\n<h3>D
 ate and time</h3><p>Wednesday 27 May 2026\, 9.30am – 5pm</p><h3>Location
 </h3><p>Monash College\, 750 Collins St\, Docklands VIC 3008</p><h3>Overvi
 ew</h3><p>The Economics of Mixed Systems short course introduces participa
 nts to the economic principles underlying Australia's complex public-priva
 te health care system. Topics include understanding the role of private he
 alth insurance\, consumer demand for public and private care\, waiting tim
 e dynamics\, supply-side factors such as physician dual practice and cream
 -skimming\, and contemporary issues facing the sector.</p><h3>Learning out
 comes</h3><p>At the end of the course\, participants will be able to:</p><
 ul><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Understand how economis
 ts conceptualise demand for public and private health care\, including the
  costs of waiting.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Eva
 luate the rationale for—and effectiveness of—private health insurance 
 incentives.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Analyse su
 pply-side dynamics including dual practice\, co-location\, and cream-skimm
 ing behaviour.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Apply e
 conomic thinking to assess intended and unintended effects of policy inter
 ventions.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Critically a
 ssess claims about the role of private health in relieving public system p
 ressures.</li></ul><h3>Who should attend?</h3><p>The course is designed fo
 r those seeking to apply economic concepts and evidence to current health 
 policy issues. No prior economics knowledge is required\; material is pres
 ented in a non-technical way with opportunities for group discussion.</p><
 p>Suitable for:</p><ul><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Pub
 lic servants and health policy analysts.</li><li aria-level="1" style="lis
 t-style-type:disc">Health service managers across public and private secto
 rs.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Health professiona
 ls.</li><li aria-level="1" style="list-style-type:disc">Consultants in hea
 lth and human services.</li></ul><h3>Cost</h3><ul><li>$950AUD for a single
  course\, or $1800AUD for the two-course package. <ul><li><strong>Volume d
 iscounts are also available for more than three participants</strong> from
  the same organisation. Please <a href="https://www.monash.edu/business/ch
 e/contact-us">contact us</a> directly for further information about how to
  take advantage of the volume discounts.</li></ul><p>Fees include attendan
 ce at a full day of face-to-face seminars and group work\, catered lunch\,
  morning and afternoon tea.</p></li></ul><h3>Teaching staff</h3><div style
 ="overflow: auto"><h4><a href="https://research.monash.edu/en/persons/tere
 nce-cheng"><img alt="Terence Cheng" height="175" src="https://www.monash.e
 du/__data/assets/image/0009/3888360/sq_Terence_Cheng.jpg" style="float: ri
 ght\; margin: 1em 0px 1em 1em\;" width="175"/>Associate Professor Terence 
 Cheng</a>\, Theme Lead (Health Financing and Market Organisation)\, Centre
  for Health Economics\, Monash Business School</h4><p>A/Prof Cheng is an e
 xpert on the economics of public and private health systems. He has publis
 hed extensively in leading international journals on topics covering publi
 c and private healthcare and insurance\, medical labour markets\, internat
 ional markets for healthcare\, and health and well-being. He is currently 
 on the editorial board of the international journal <em>Health Systems and
  Reform</em>.</p><p>A/Prof Cheng has held academic and research appointmen
 ts at universities in Australia\, Singapore and the United States. He retu
 rned to Australia in 2024 after a three-year stint as a Research Scientist
  of Health Systems Research and Policy at the Harvard T.H. Chan School of 
 Public Health. He has consulted for the World Health Organisation\, the Wo
 rld Bank and various government departments in Australia.</p></div><h3>Pro
 gram</h3><table id="table04982"><thead><tr><th id="table04982r1c1">Time</t
 h><th id="table04982r1c2">Session</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td headers=
 "table04982r1c1">\n        9.00am\n      </td><td headers="table04982r1c2"
 >\n        Arrival and registration\n      </td></tr><tr><td headers="tabl
 e04982r1c1">\n        9.30 – 10.40am\n      </td><td headers="table04982
 r1c2">\n        Overview and Australia's hybrid system through a comparati
 ve lens\n      </td></tr><tr><td headers="table04982r1c1">\n        10.40 
 – 11.15am\n      </td><td headers="table04982r1c2">\n        Morning tea
  break\n      </td></tr><tr><td headers="table04982r1c1">\n        11.15am
  – 12.30pm\n      </td><td headers="table04982r1c2">\n        Understand
 ing consumer demand & group work\n      </td></tr><tr><td headers="table04
 982r1c1">\n        12.30 – 1.10pm\n      </td><td headers="table04982r1c
 2">\n        Lunch break\n      </td></tr><tr><td headers="table04982r1c1"
 >\n        1.10 – 2.20pm\n      </td><td headers="table04982r1c2">\n    
